### Describe the Bug

The default `parentFilterOptions` that `plugin-nested-docs` attaches to the `parent` field:

```js
{
  id: { not_equals: id },
  'breadcrumbs.doc': { not_in: [id] },
}
```

**excludes nothing beyond the doc itself on the Postgres adapter.** The parent picker offers every descendant of the doc being edited, so an editor can select one and create a circular reference from the default admin UI.

Root cause is query semantics, not stale breadcrumbs: on `db-postgres`, breadcrumbs live in a separate rows table (`<collection>_breadcrumbs`), and `not_in` against a hasMany sub-field matches a doc when **any** joined row satisfies the condition, rather than when **all** rows do. Since `populateBreadcrumbs` always writes a self-row (and usually other ancestors), every doc has at least one breadcrumb row whose `doc` differs from the edited id — so the `not_in` clause matches every doc, always.

This was previously reported in #13080 (July 2025), which was closed for inactivity without a fix and is now locked. It still reproduces at 3.80.0 and `packages/plugin-nested-docs/src/fields/parentFilterOptions.ts` is unchanged on current `main`.

### Reproduction Steps

1. Postgres adapter; a collection (e.g. `categories`) with `nestedDocsPlugin({ collections: ['categories'] })`, no overrides.
2. Create `cat 2` (root), `cat 1` under `cat 2`, `cat 4` under `cat 1`, `cat 5` under `cat 4`. Breadcrumbs are freshly written and correct — verified directly in the DB: every descendant has a breadcrumbs row with `doc_id` = cat 2's id (391 below).
3. Run the exact query the picker issues when editing `cat 2`:

```
GET /api/categories?where[id][not_equals]=391&where[breadcrumbs.doc][not_in][0]=391
```

**Expected:** only non-descendants.
**Actual:** all four descendants are returned — each with breadcrumbs plainly containing `"doc": 391` in the very same response:

```json
{ "id": 394, "title": "cat 5", "breadcrumbs": [ { "doc": 391, ... }, { "doc": 390, ... }, { "doc": 393, ... }, { "doc": 394, ... } ], ... }
```

The same happens with a pages-style setup and custom filterOptions using the same `'breadcrumbs.doc': { not_in: [id] }` clause.

### Impact

This is the protection that's supposed to make circular references hard to create from the admin UI. With it silently inert, any editor can pick a descendant as parent, and the save then meets the missing cycle guards in `getParents`/`resaveChildren` (#16517): in our case a single admin save spun forever inside an open transaction (details in https://github.com/payloadcms/payload/issues/16517#issuecomment-5196001279). The three bugs compose: broken filter lets the cycle in, missing cycle detection makes it hang.

### Suggested direction

Either fix `not_in` hasMany semantics in the adapters (match when *no* row equals), or make the plugin's default filter not depend on them. We ship the latter as a workaround — an async `filterOptions` that walks the real stored subtree by parent links and excludes by plain id (also immune to breadcrumbs lagging reality):

```ts
filterOptions: async ({ id, req }) => {
  if (id == null) return true;
  const excluded = new Set([id]);
  let frontier = [id];
  while (frontier.length > 0) {
    const { docs } = await req.payload.find({
      collection: 'categories',
      depth: 0, limit: 0, pagination: false, req,
      select: {},
      where: { parent: { in: frontier } },
    });
    frontier = [];
    for (const child of docs) {
      if (!excluded.has(child.id)) { excluded.add(child.id); frontier.push(child.id); }
    }
  }
  return { id: { not_in: [...excluded] } };
};
